WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:01.060
Good morning, Hank.

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:02.060
It's Tuesday.

00:00:02.060 --> 00:00:04.250
The Paper Towns movie finished shooting a
couple days ago so I am home.

00:00:04.250 --> 00:00:08.390
I'm tired and a little bit sick, and as you
can probably hear, my voice has been rode

00:00:08.390 --> 00:00:12.620
hard and put away wet, but I'm happy to be
home and am so grateful to everybody who worked

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.270
so hard on the Paper Towns movie.

00:00:14.270 --> 00:00:17.980
So Hank, this is a map of New York created
by the General Drafting Company for the gas

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:18.980
station Esso.

00:00:18.980 --> 00:00:22.820
I suppose I should explain for our younger
viewers that this is essentially like a static

00:00:22.820 --> 00:00:26.529
version of Google Maps printed on a very large
sheet of paper.

00:00:26.529 --> 00:00:30.390
But anyway, this particular map is interesting
because where— wait, where are you?

00:00:30.390 --> 00:00:31.390
Ah!

00:00:31.390 --> 00:00:32.390
There you are.

00:00:32.390 --> 00:00:34.860
So this map is kind of famous to cartography
nerds and also to readers of Paper Towns because

00:00:34.860 --> 00:00:39.950
just north of a little town called Rockland,
New York, it contains Agloe, New York.

00:00:39.950 --> 00:00:44.160
Agloe was a town made up by the two guys who
created this map, Ernest G Alpers and Otto

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:45.160
Lindbergh.

00:00:45.160 --> 00:00:46.850
They took their initials together, mixed them
up, and made Agloe.

00:00:46.850 --> 00:00:49.880
And then they put their little fake town in
the middle of nowhere, at the intersection

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:51.550
of two dirt roads in the Catskills.

00:00:51.550 --> 00:00:53.960
They did this to protect their copyright.

00:00:53.960 --> 00:00:57.020
Mapmakers have always created these little
'paper towns' or 'paper streets' so if they

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:00.430
see them on someone else's map, they'll know
that they've been robbed.

00:01:00.430 --> 00:01:04.170
Anyway, flash forward like forty years after
the first General Drafting map with Algoe,

00:01:04.170 --> 00:01:08.289
New York on it, Rand McNally makes a map with
Agloe, New York on it.

00:01:08.289 --> 00:01:11.850
And when General Drafting calls Rand McNally
to be like "We're gonna sue you" Rand McNally

00:01:11.850 --> 00:01:13.399
says, "NONONONO!

00:01:13.399 --> 00:01:15.340
Agloe is real!"

00:01:15.340 --> 00:01:19.420
Because people kept going to that intersection
of two dirt roads in the middle of nowhere,

00:01:19.420 --> 00:01:23.460
expecting a place called Agloe, someone built
a place called Agloe.

00:01:23.460 --> 00:01:27.310
Essentially, Hank, the story of Agloe, New
York is the story of a fiction that became

00:01:27.310 --> 00:01:28.310
real.

00:01:28.310 --> 00:01:30.859
Now, I would argue that this happens to fictions
and constructions all the time, like I remember

00:01:30.859 --> 00:01:34.889
one time in college I told one of my professors
that things that are constructed aren't really

00:01:34.889 --> 00:01:39.210
real, and my professor was like, "Well, this
chair is constructed, but if I hit you with

00:01:39.210 --> 00:01:40.999
it, it is going to hurt."

00:01:40.999 --> 00:01:44.960
The truth is, Hank, the way we imagine the
world does end up shaping reality.

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:48.789
And the book Paper Towns is about how we hurt
ourselves and each other when we imagine other

00:01:48.789 --> 00:01:51.450
people as less than human or more than human.

00:01:51.450 --> 00:01:54.819
But I guess what interests me most is the
way that our incomplete imaginings of people

00:01:54.819 --> 00:01:59.399
and their incomplete imaginings of us end
up shaping our lives really profoundly.

00:01:59.399 --> 00:02:02.951
So anyway, back in 2008 I published a story
about how like the merely imagined can, in

00:02:02.951 --> 00:02:05.579
my opinion, become really real.

00:02:05.579 --> 00:02:09.060
And then Agloe, New York, which had originally
been fictional and then became real and then

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:13.750
sort of fell into disrepair and disappeared
again, kind of became real again.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:18.319
Readers started making pilgrimages to Agloe,
they found the site of the former Agloe general

00:02:18.319 --> 00:02:22.292
store, one of them even made a fake historical
marker which, I guess, come to think of it,

00:02:22.292 --> 00:02:24.569
is kind of a real historical marker?

00:02:24.569 --> 00:02:28.660
And then, six years later, largely thanks
to readers continuing to care about Q and

00:02:28.660 --> 00:02:34.489
Margo and Agloe, people in Hollywood decided
to make a movie, and it all became real again,

00:02:34.489 --> 00:02:35.489
in a way.

00:02:35.489 --> 00:02:39.209
I got to meet all of these people that I'd
imagined and go inside of their houses and

00:02:39.209 --> 00:02:40.989
visit Agloe, New York with them.

00:02:40.989 --> 00:02:45.860
Hank, the journey of this to this has been
the weirdest and most wonderful part of what

00:02:45.860 --> 00:02:49.090
has been a very weird and wonderful year for
me, personally.

00:02:49.090 --> 00:02:52.819
So I just wanted to take a second to thank
everyone who's made Paper Towns real by virtue

00:02:52.819 --> 00:02:55.579
of reading it and, in turn, made Agloe real
again.

00:02:55.579 --> 00:02:59.270
Of course, I know that Agloe will, again,
cease to exist someday, as everything does.

00:02:59.270 --> 00:03:02.750
But I think it's pretty cool that, together,
we've made it real for awhile.

00:03:02.750 --> 00:03:03.750
Thanks for that.

00:03:03.750 --> 00:03:06.019
I can't even begin to explain what a gift
it's been for me.

00:03:06.019 --> 00:03:07.480
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

